From 670a753e05f85180d71402f3d8b6dbda13ae55fa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SteveDeFacto <5148995+SteveDeFacto@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 09:56:09 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Manager: one purchased share sizes the VM's processors as
 well as its RAM

Every NucBox app VM ran 1 VP at 100% (Reserve 0) whatever its share, while app RAM already
followed floor(pool*share). The manager now sizes CPU from the SAME cpuShare (cpu-share.mjs):
VPs = max(catalog vcpus, ceil(pool*share)), and an exact per-VP Msvm_ProcessorSettingData
Limit = Reservation in Hyper-V's percent/1000 units (16 LP: 1% -> 1 VP @16000, 21% -> 4 @84000,
25% -> 4 @100000, 13% -> 3 @69333). A separate pre-start processor script sets it on the
never-started VM, reads it back and fails the start (VM removed by Id) on any mismatch; the
define script is unchanged. Pool = the node's rule NODE_VCPUS || os.cpus().length, a malformed
explicit value refuses startup, and it must fit Hyper-V's LogicalProcessorCount before any
deployment spawns. Admission never oversubscribes the pool; recovered partitions keep it 503.
The readiness witness keeps its fixed launch. Records state the granted, read-back and
realizable allocation, including the guest's online VPs and the measured monitor's catalog
cgroup cap (a blocker until a guest image honours ENCLAVE_GUEST_CPU_ALLOWANCE, off by default).
The catalog policy, AppID and attestation statements are unchanged.
